{{smaller block|The places [in Domesday] which speak of the ''antecessor'' and of the rights derived from him to the present owner are endless. {{right|''E. A. Freeman'', Norman Conquest, V. 11.}}}}

'''antechamber''' (an′tẹ̄-chām″bėr), ''n.'' [< ''ante-'' + ''chamber''.] A chamber or an apartment through which access is had to a principal apartment, and in which persons wait for audience. Formerly also spelled ''antichamber''.
{{smaller block|<poem>They both were cast into the dungeon’s gloom,
That dismal ''antechamber'' of the tomb.</poem>
{{right|''Longfellow'', Torquemada.}}}}

'''antechapel''' (an′tē-chap″el), ''n.'' [< ''ante-'' + ''chapel''.] An apartment, vestibule, porch, or the like, before the entrance to a chapel; the narthex of a chapel.

'''Antechinomys''' (an-te-kī′nō-mis), ''n.'' [NL. (Krefft), < ''ant-'' for ''anti-'' + ''Echinomys'', q. v.] A genus of very small insectivorous marsupials, of the family ''Dasyuridæ''. {{smaller|''A. lanigera'', inhabiting central portions of Australia, is about 3 inches long and of a mouse-gray color above and white below. Its tail is about 5 inches long, and tufted at the tip. A naked space surrounds the teats, but there is no distinct pouch.}}

'''antechoir''' (an′tē-kwīr), ''n.'' [< ''ante-'' + ''choir''.] In ''arch.'', a space, more or less inclosed, in front of the choir of a church; a portion of the nave adjoining the choir-screen and separated from the rest of the nave by a railing. Also called ''fore-choir''. ''Audsley.''

