'''antelopian''' (an-tẹ-lō′pi-a̤n), ''a.'' Same as ''antelopine.''

'''Antelopidæ''' (an-tẹ-lop′i-dē), ''n. pl.'' Same as ''Antilopidæ.''

'''Antelopinæ''', ''n. pl.'' Same as ''Antilopinæ.''

'''antelopine''' (an′tẹ-lō-pin), ''a.'' [< ''antelope'' + ''-ine''<sup>1</sup>.] Pertaining to the antelope. An equivalent form is ''antelopian.''

'''antelucan''' (an-tẹ-lū′kan), ''a.'' [< L. ''antelucanus'', < ''ante'', before, + ''lux'' (''luc-''), light: see ''lucid.''] Occurring before daylight; preceding the dawn. {{smaller|Specifically applied to assemblies of Christians held in ancient times before daylight, at first to escape persecution, and afterward from motives of devotion or convenience.}}
{{smaller block|This practice of . . . ''antelucan'' worship, possibly having reference to the ineffable mystery of the resurrection.
{{right|''De Quincey'', Essenes, i.}}}}

'''ante lucem''' (an′tē lū′sem). [L.: ''ante'', before; ''lucem'', acc. of ''lux'', light: see ''ante-'' and ''lucid.'']

